  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 AM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nToday, the local area will be under low-amplitude upper level \nridging. A subtle shortwave will move through the area this \nafternoon. This feature may provide enough lift to produce a few \nsprinkles however, dry area below 10kft will most likely relegate \nprecipitation (10-15%) to virga. This feature will provide mostly \ncloudy to cloudy skies today however, the low amplitude ridging and \nweak WAA will still allow highs to rise into the lower 70s. Another \nstronger shortwave rounding the upper ridge will move through the \ncentral Plains tonight and approach the local area. However, models \nsuggest this feature weakens as it approaches and dry air below 10kft \ncontinues to be in place and may actually be further reinforced by a \nsurface high pressure ridge building back westward from the Ohio and \nmid-Mississippi River Valleys. Consequently, precipitation chances \nremain 10-15% tonight over the eastern Kansas and western Missouri. \nTomorrow, surface high pressure continues to build back into the \narea as upper level ridging become more amplified over the central \nCONUS in response to a upper level trough digging south along the \nwest coast. Despite weak mixing, height rises will aid in highs \nrising into the low to mid 70s. Thursday will present similar \nconditions to Wednesday with continued height rises as upper level \nridging builds over the central CONUS. This will push highs into the \nmid to upper 70s.\n\nThe gradual warming trend through midweek continues Friday and \nSaturday as the upper level ridge axis moves directly over the area. \nThis will provide continued height rises pushing highs into the mid \n70s to lower 80s by Saturday. Saturday into Saturday night a upper \nlevel trough will across the Canadian Plains forcing a sagging cold \nfront towards the area. This front is not expected to reach the area \nSaturday night but a few showers ahead of it may be possible across \nfar northwestern Missouri. Sunday is expected to be dry and warm as \nthe cold front stall north and west of the forecast area with highs \nremaining in the 70s. Sunday into Sunday night a upper level trough \nover the Four Corners region will weaken and move into the central \nPlains. This feature will finally drive the cold front into the area \non Monday bringing the chance for showers (15-30%) however, moisture \nwill be meager. Highs Monday will be in the mid 60s to lower 70s. \nSurface high pressure will build into the area behind the front on \nTuesday cooling temperatures nearer to seasonal normals in the 60s.",
